This interpolation is taking place in sRGB space and not linear space - in other words, it is not gamma-corrected. [Web compensates for this](https://github.com/ppy/osu-web/blob/71fbab8936d79a7929d13854f5e854b4f383b236/resources/assets/lib/utils/beatmap-helper.ts#L26). This means that the colour values are potentially slightly off.

I checked colours for [this mapset](https://osu.ppy.sh/beatmapsets/1130581#osu/2396095) against web and got the following:

![2021-08-03-235142_268x386_scrot](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/20418176/128091532-3a9cb514-f7b5-4c29-ab15-642d19059cfe.png)

`(lazer before)` colours are this PR. `(lazer after)` colours are this PR after performing gamma correction by interpolating in linear space:

```diff
diff --git a/osu.Game/Utils/ColourUtils.cs b/osu.Game/Utils/ColourUtils.cs
index 515963971d..b42e10cecb 100644
--- a/osu.Game/Utils/ColourUtils.cs
+++ b/osu.Game/Utils/ColourUtils.cs
@@ -2,6 +2,7 @@
 // See the LICENCE file in the repository root for full licence text.
 
 using System.Collections.Generic;
+using osu.Framework.Extensions.Color4Extensions;
 using osu.Framework.Utils;
 using osuTK.Graphics;
 
@@ -28,7 +29,10 @@ public static Color4 SampleFromLinearGradient(IReadOnlyList<(float position, Col
                 if (point >= endStop.position)
                     continue;
 
-                return Interpolation.ValueAt(point, startStop.colour, endStop.colour, startStop.position, endStop.position);
+                var startLinear = startStop.colour.ToLinear();
+                var endLinear = endStop.colour.ToLinear();
+
+                return Interpolation.ValueAt(point, startLinear, endLinear, startStop.position, endStop.position).ToSRGB();
             }
 
             return gradient[^1].colour;
```

While not perfect still, the `(after)` numbers are a lot closer (discrepancies of at most 1-2 for each colour component).

Possibly `Interpolation.ValueAt()` for colours should be doing the linear conversion automatically, in framework. No strong opinions here.
